Clamp the recording scale factor to the core's actual resolution.

scale_factor is a plain multiplier, which only makes sense if every core renders
at the same size. A factor tuned for 240p pixel art -- where pre-scaling defeats
4:2:0 chroma subsampling and costs almost nothing in bitrate -- is badly wrong for
a core already running at 480p.

Measured: PSX Tekken 3 outputs 368x480, and 3x made that 1104x1440. That is 3.1x
the pixels of an NES capture at the same factor, and it pinned the encoder at 100%
of its bitrate on exactly the detailed 3D content that needs bits the most. The
stored frame was also portrait (0.77:1) and stretched to 4:3 on playback via SAR,
so those extra vertical lines were squeezed away by the player anyway.

Clamping happens here rather than in the record preset because this is the only
place the core's real geometry is known, and it can change mid-game: Mega Drive
titles switch between 256 and 320 wide, so no preset value can be right for all of
a session.

Integer division floors, which is what we want -- factors stay whole, since a
fractional scale can yield odd dimensions that H.264 cannot represent in yuv420p.
It never scales below 1, so a core larger than the cap is recorded at its native
size rather than shrunk. Every 240p core keeps the factor it had.

diff --git a/record/drivers/record_ffmpeg.c b/record/drivers/record_ffmpeg.c
index cfe0b6acd0..24254a1368 100644
--- a/record/drivers/record_ffmpeg.c
+++ b/record/drivers/record_ffmpeg.c
@@ -393,6 +393,13 @@ static bool ffmpeg_init_audio(ffmpeg_t *handle, const char *audio_resampler)
    return true;
 }
 
+/* Ceiling for a pre-scaled recording frame. 720 matches the device panel and
+ * leaves the 240p cores untouched at 3x (224 -> 672, 240 -> 720), while a core
+ * already at 480p is recorded natively instead of being tripled. The width cap
+ * is a second guard for unusually wide arcade resolutions. */
+#define JW_RECORD_MAX_HEIGHT 720u
+#define JW_RECORD_MAX_WIDTH  1280u
+
 static bool ffmpeg_init_video(ffmpeg_t *handle)
 {
    size_t size;
@@ -481,8 +488,45 @@ static bool ffmpeg_init_video(ffmpeg_t *handle)
    /* Useful to set scale_factor to 2 for chroma subsampled formats to
     * maintain full chroma resolution. (Or just use 4:4:4 or RGB ...)
     */
-   param->out_width  = (float)param->out_width  * params->scale_factor;
-   param->out_height = (float)param->out_height * params->scale_factor;
+   {
+      /* scale_factor is a plain multiplier, which is only sensible if every core
+       * renders at the same size. They do not. A factor tuned for 240p pixel art
+       * (where pre-scaling defeats 4:2:0 chroma subsampling and costs almost
+       * nothing) is badly wrong for a core already running at 480p: PSX Tekken 3
+       * outputs 368x480, and 3x made that 1104x1440 -- 3.1x the pixels of an NES
+       * capture at the same factor, which pinned the encoder at 100% of its
+       * bitrate on exactly the detailed 3D content that needs bits the most.
+       *
+       * Clamp the factor to whatever keeps the encode inside a sane frame instead.
+       * Done here rather than in the preset because this is the only place the
+       * core's real geometry is known, and it can change mid-game -- Mega Drive
+       * titles switch between 256 and 320 wide, so a preset cannot get it right.
+       *
+       * Integer division floors, which is what we want: factors stay whole, since
+       * a fractional scale can yield odd dimensions that H.264 cannot represent in
+       * yuv420p. Never scales below 1, so a core larger than the cap is recorded
+       * at its native size rather than shrunk. */
+      unsigned limit_h = param->out_height
+         ? (JW_RECORD_MAX_HEIGHT / param->out_height) : 1u;
+      unsigned limit_w = param->out_width
+         ? (JW_RECORD_MAX_WIDTH  / param->out_width)  : 1u;
+      unsigned limit   = limit_h < limit_w ? limit_h : limit_w;
+      float    scale   = params->scale_factor;
+
+      if (limit < 1u)
+         limit = 1u;
+      if (scale > (float)limit)
+         scale = (float)limit;
+      if (scale < 1.0f)
+         scale = 1.0f;
+
+      if (scale != params->scale_factor)
+         RARCH_LOG("[Recording] Clamped scale %.1f -> %.1f for %ux%u source.\n",
+               params->scale_factor, scale, param->out_width, param->out_height);
+
+      param->out_width  = (float)param->out_width  * scale;
+      param->out_height = (float)param->out_height * scale;
+   }
 
    video->codec->codec_type          = AVMEDIA_TYPE_VIDEO;
    video->codec->width               = param->out_width;
